    "tasteProfileDe": "Harzig-waldig (Rosmarin), w\u00fcrzig-herb (Thymian), pfeffrig-frisch (Basilikum), warm (Oregano).",
    "scienceEn": "Culinary chemistry divides herbs into two fundamental gastronomic categories: 1) Woody, resinous herbs (Rosemary, Thyme, Winter Savory): Native to dry rocky soils, they protect themselves with thick waxy cuticles containing heavy, heat-stable essential oils (rosmarinic acid, carvacrol, thymol). They thrive in hot oil and long cooking times. 2) Tender leaf herbs (Sweet Basil, Chervil, Tarragon): High in water and volatile monoterpenes (linalool, eugenol). High heat destroys their aromatic structure within seconds; they must be torn and added raw just before plating.",
    "scienceDe": "Die Gastronomie unterscheidet zwei Welten: 1) Robuste Holz- und Nadelkr\u00e4uter (Rosmarin, Thymian, Salbei, Oregano): Wachsen auf kargen Felsb\u00f6den, haben derbe Schutzwachsschichten und hitzestabile atherische \u00d6le (Thymol, Cineol). Sie m\u00fcssen in hei\u00dfem Fett oder Schmorgerichten mitgekocht werden, um ihr Aroma ans Fett abzugeben.\\n2) Zarte Blattkr\u00e4uter (Basilikum, Kerbel, Estragon): Hitze zerst\u00f6rt die zarten Aromamolek\u00fcle (Linalool) in Sekunden; Basilikum darf erst ganz zum Schluss frisch \u00fcber Pasta oder Caprese gerupft werden.",
