    "scienceEn": "The critical kitchen chemistry of cooking oils: 1) Smoke Point: The temperature at which volatile free fatty acids break down into toxic blue smoke and carcinogenic acrolein. Unrefined virgin oils (flaxseed 107\u00b0C, extra virgin olive oil 190\u00b0C, cold-pressed rapeseed 110\u00b0C) must never be used for searing steaks or deep-frying! 2) Refined oils (refined high-oleic sunflower, refined canola, peanut oil) have free acids stripped out, raising smoke points above 225-240\u00b0C. 3) Omega-3 vs Omega-6: Cold-pressed rapeseed oil has an optimal 1:2 ratio of Omega-3 to Omega-6; standard sunflower oil is high in inflammatory Omega-6.",
    "scienceDe": "Das unverzichtbare K\u00fcchenwissen \u00fcber Speise\u00f6le: 1) Der Rauchpunkt: Die Temperatur, bei der Fetts\u00e4uren zerfallen und giftiges Acrolein verdampft. Kaltgepresste \u00d6le (Lein\u00f6l 107\u00b0C, kaltgepresstes Raps\u00f6l 120\u00b0C, Walnuss\u00f6l 160\u00b0C) d\u00fcrfen NIEMALS zum hei\u00dfen Anbraten genutzt werden! 2) Raffinierte \u00d6le (raffiniertes Raps\u00f6l, Erdnuss\u00f6l, High-Oleic-Sonnenblumen\u00f6l) sind von Tr\u00fcbstoffen befreit, rauchen erst ab 220-240\u00b0C und sind ideal f\u00fcr Pfanne und Fritteuse. 3) Kaltgepresstes Steirisches K\u00fcrbiskern\u00f6l g.g.A. ist ein reines Kalt\u00f6l \u2013 bei Hitze verbrennen die edlen Aromen sofort!",
    "scienceExplainedEn": "The critical kitchen chemistry of cooking oils: 1) Smoke Point: The temperature at which volatile free fatty acids break down into toxic blue smoke and carcinogenic acrolein. Unrefined virgin oils (flaxseed 107\u00b0C, extra virgin olive oil 190\u00b0C, cold-pressed rapeseed 110\u00b0C) must never be used for searing steaks or deep-frying! 2) Refined oils (refined high-oleic sunflower, refined canola, peanut oil) have free acids stripped out, raising smoke points above 225-240\u00b0C. 3) Omega-3 vs Omega-6: Cold-pressed rapeseed oil has an optimal 1:2 ratio of Omega-3 to Omega-6; standard sunflower oil is high in inflammatory Omega-6.",
